 "notes": "Raw agreement 79.8% (bootstrap 67.0% to 89.0%); deciding 66.1% (59.6% to 78.0%); area-shuffle null 95% range 40.4% to 58.7%. Agreement by kind of pair (deciding): {'air, traffic or noise with anything': {'n': 27, 'agree_deciding': 10, 'agree_raw': 10}, 'behaviour with anything else': {'n': 26, 'agree_deciding': 14, 'agree_raw': 21}, 'health outcome with health outcome': {'n': 41, 'agree_deciding': 34, 'agree_raw': 41}, 'social or housing condition with anything else': {'n': 15, 'agree_deciding': 14, 'agree_raw': 15}}. Without the five previously analysed pairs: 66.3%. Exploratory: without the negation rule 66.4% of 125 pairs; partialling population density as well as poverty 65.3% of 101. Correction to the registration text: 50 of the 56 indicators have a concept, not 48. The bootstrap interval is not centred on the estimate because resampling neighbourhoods with replacement creates ties. Several EHDP measures have no recent UHF42 release (pedestrian injuries and heat ED visits 2014, infant deaths 2013, housing conditions 2017, tree shade 2014, walkability 2011). Traffic volume is published as vehicle miles without an area denominator. The negation rule has false positives ('non-cystic fibrosis', 'compared to lowest quartile'). Individual pairs are exploratory.\n\nAfter the referee (13 Nov 2026): the referee recommended unsettled. The verdict is kept as confirmed, because the registered support condition is written on the agreement rate itself (at least 60% and above the 97.5th percentile of the area-shuffle null, 58.7%), the rate is 66.1%, and rule 1 binds. What is confirmed is narrower than the registered statement reads. The test shows that these indicators, coded so that higher means more of an adverse condition, are positively correlated across neighbourhoods after partialling poverty. It does not show that the graph's signs carry information about the city. Of the 109 literature signs 105 are positive, and a rule that ignores the literature and always answers 'positive' scores 70 of 109 (64.2%) against the literature's 72 (66.1%). The four pairs with a negative literature sign split three to one (Fisher exact p = 0.13), and the registered sign-shuffle null gives p = 0.14. The exposure-with-outcome pairs, where the literature's sign says something about a cause, agree at 29 of 56 (51.8%), which is chance; the 41 pairs of two health outcomes agree at 82.9%, the same as always-positive. The lower bootstrap bound (59.6%) is below the 60% threshold and 5% of resamples fall below it, so the condition is met on a point estimate about 1.3 standard errors above its threshold (rule 10). An always-positive rule passes the 60% threshold in 78% of resamples, so a literature with no information beyond valence would usually have been confirmed. Seven indicators each appear in 12 to 19 pairs; leaving one indicator out moves the rate between 63.5% and 70.0%. A pair qualifies only with three studies and 80% consistency, and published associations between two adverse conditions are almost all positive, so the qualifying set holds almost no negative signs by construction. The stance is now measured against the always-positive rate and not against 50%.",
